"Find out why the hydrogen Balmer lines are weak in both cool and hot stars and strongest near {#} {#} K, and how the pressure in a star’s atmosphere moves that peak.",
"<strong>Balmer lines.</strong> A hydrogen atom absorbs a Balmer line (Hα at {#} nm, Hβ at {#} nm, and so on) when its electron jumps up from level n = {#}. Only atoms already on n = {#} can absorb them. The energy of level n is \\(E_n = -13.6\\ \\text{eV}/n^2\\), so \\(E_2 - E_1 = 10.2\\) eV.",
"<strong>Boltzmann: excitation.</strong> In a gas at temperature \\(T\\), the ratio of atoms on two levels is",
"\\[\\frac{N_2}{N_1} = \\frac{g_2}{g_1}\\,e^{-(E_2 - E_1)/kT},\\]",
"where \\(g_n = 2n^2\\) is the number of states on level n, so \\(g_2/g_1 = 4\\). Use \\(k = 8.617\\times10^{-5}\\) eV/K.",
"<strong>Saha: ionization.</strong> Hot atoms also lose their electron. The ratio of ionized to neutral hydrogen is",
"\\[\\frac{N_{II}}{N_I} = \\frac{2kT\\,Z_{II}}{P_e\\,Z_I}\\left(\\frac{2\\pi m_e kT}{h^2}\\right)^{3/2} e^{-\\chi/kT},\\]",
"with \\(\\chi = 13.6\\) eV the ionization energy, \\(P_e\\) the electron pressure, and partition functions \\(Z_I \\approx 2\\) (the neutral atom, ground level only) and \\(Z_{II} = 1\\) (a bare proton). More free electrons (a larger \\(P_e\\)) recapture more protons, so they keep more hydrogen neutral.",
"<strong>Putting them together.</strong> The share of all hydrogen that sits on n = {#} is",
"\\[\\frac{N_2}{N_{\\text{total}}} = \\frac{N_2/N_1}{1 + N_2/N_1}\\cdot\\frac{1}{1 + N_{II}/N_I}.\\]",
